WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.080
Hi Veronica.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:02.250
Hi Steve.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:09.210
Today, I'm gonna talk with  Veronika Mark
of Moscow about just how much can you

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:15.210
learn on your own in a place where the
language you're learning is not spoken.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.350
Now, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:20.850
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.675 --> 00:00:24.675
If you're following me on a
podcast, please leave a review.

00:00:24.675 --> 00:00:25.395
I appreciate it.

00:00:25.634 --> 00:00:27.375
So hello, Veronika.

00:00:27.765 --> 00:00:28.065
Hi.

00:00:29.355 --> 00:00:33.105
We are going to speak in English
because Veronika's English is amazing.

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:34.394
Absolutely amazing.

00:00:35.495 --> 00:00:39.905
Veronika has a website, a YouTube
channel called English with Veronika

00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:42.675
or with Veronika Mark, which is it now?

00:00:43.035 --> 00:00:43.635
With Veronika?

00:00:44.185 --> 00:00:45.745
With Veronika Mark, yeah you were right.

00:00:45.905 --> 00:00:47.175
With Veronika Mark, okay.

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:52.739
And we just had a conversation
in English, and her English is,

00:00:52.889 --> 00:00:55.010
I would say flawless, flawless.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.129
Now, would I say I would mistake
her for a native speaker?

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.959
Not quite, but almost.

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:06.030
And the use of words, the way you put
words together is completely native

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:09.210
speaker, uh, intonation, everything.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.880
There's just very few little
things that give you away.

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:16.020
But what I find very interesting
with Veronika is you have basically

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.560
lived your whole life in Moscow.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.530
Yeah in russia.

00:01:19.589 --> 00:01:19.830
Yeah.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.089
I I'm not from Moscow.

00:01:21.089 --> 00:01:22.399
So I lived, yeah.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:27.869
I lived in a different city until I
was, uh, 17 and then I moved to Moscow.

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:28.530
Yeah.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:29.160
Okay.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.420
So we often hear people say, you
know, to learn the language really

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.380
well, you have to move to the
country where the language is spoken.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.895
And so, and you are living
proof that that's not necessary.

00:01:41.925 --> 00:01:45.585
So how did you go about achieving
such a high level of English

00:01:45.645 --> 00:01:47.795
without really leaving Russia?

00:01:48.435 --> 00:01:48.785
Yeah.

00:01:49.135 --> 00:01:51.059
So thank you for your question.

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:54.750
Uh, when I was in school, like in
Russia, it's mandatory for every

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.919
student, for every kid to start
learning English at school as a,

00:01:59.039 --> 00:02:00.389
as the first foreign language.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:04.830
And, uh, obviously when I was a kid, like
I was eight years old, I didn't really

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.630
care about foreign languages at all.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.660
And for me it was just another
subject that I didn't like.

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:15.660
And I feel like the moment that I realized
that I actually really enjoy English was

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:22.435
when I was, it was my, uh, yeah, I think
I was in high school, my junior year in

00:02:22.435 --> 00:02:27.565
high school, and I was just so excited
to learn, to start learning English.

00:02:28.075 --> 00:02:31.435
And first of all, I only
focused on the input

00:02:31.695 --> 00:02:35.670
I was just listening to
something, reading books, just...

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:40.200
first, I started with some stupid
books for kids, because for me it

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.220
was really entertaining and they
were actually really interesting.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.600
And, uh, I had a, I had an
English teacher, she was Russian.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.829
So, but she had a very good level
of English and she helped me a lot.

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:57.000
She was always motivating me
and she, she was just amazing

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.530
and I'm really grateful for her.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:04.160
And, uh, then there was time when
I had to prepare to go to college.

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:09.049
I had to take some tests and exams,
and I decided to take English as my

00:03:09.049 --> 00:03:11.570
exam because in Russia we can choose.

00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:18.135
Um, so, and then the, that is when
I really had to prepare well, and,

00:03:18.165 --> 00:03:21.855
but obviously when you prepare for
a test, it's a little bit different

00:03:21.855 --> 00:03:26.025
than what you actually need, like
to communicate with people or to

00:03:26.265 --> 00:03:27.975
just read something in English.

00:03:28.425 --> 00:03:32.055
And when I moved to Moscow,
this is when I realized that

00:03:32.505 --> 00:03:36.075
I really lacked just, yeah...

00:03:36.285 --> 00:03:41.055
I had a lot of things that I didn't know,
like culture wise and just vocabulary,

00:03:41.055 --> 00:03:43.785
my vocabulary wasn't really as great.

00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:49.425
And, uh, one of my professors, English
professors told me that I should really

00:03:49.425 --> 00:03:55.365
look into accents and think about because
my English was already pretty good.

00:03:55.365 --> 00:03:59.205
And there was one thing that
she said could be better,

00:03:59.355 --> 00:04:01.005
which is my pronunciation.

00:04:01.665 --> 00:04:05.385
Uh, because sometimes I mixed up
accents, I would say something in

00:04:05.385 --> 00:04:09.495
a British accent and then another
word would be an American accent.

00:04:10.245 --> 00:04:14.185
Then my Russian accent
obviously would be also there.

00:04:15.155 --> 00:04:18.480
And this is when I was like, okay,
now I need to really think about

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.410
improving my accent and I try to
focus more on the American accent.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:28.530
And this is when I learned all
those rules of connected speech.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.430
Um, yeah, all of those different
sounds in the American English.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:39.075
And obviously still every single
day I focused on the input because

00:04:39.075 --> 00:04:40.844
I just read a lot in English.

00:04:40.875 --> 00:04:43.605
I love reading in any language, really.

00:04:43.605 --> 00:04:45.165
So for me, it was just natural.

00:04:45.165 --> 00:04:46.665
It's just the reading in English.

00:04:47.145 --> 00:04:49.215
And I really love listening to podcasts.

00:04:49.575 --> 00:04:51.135
So I should probably add to that.

00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:53.325
So you speak English, you speak Chinese.

00:04:54.015 --> 00:04:55.395
What other languages do you speak?

00:04:56.085 --> 00:04:58.575
French at a beginner level, yeah.

00:04:58.905 --> 00:04:59.715
At a beginner level.

00:04:59.865 --> 00:05:01.115
And your Chinese is...

00:05:01.435 --> 00:05:02.645
How would you classify it?

00:05:03.205 --> 00:05:06.635
Intermediate, sometimes when I
practice more upper intermediate.

00:05:06.825 --> 00:05:07.125
Yeah.

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:08.865
Okay.

00:05:09.315 --> 00:05:13.035
And so basically when you were very
young, you weren't that interested.

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:17.104
And then at the age of 16, you become,
you, you became very interested?

00:05:17.104 --> 00:05:18.025
Yeah, very interested.

00:05:18.045 --> 00:05:20.085
And, uh, it's interesting.

00:05:20.385 --> 00:05:24.255
A lot of parents, they want their kids to
learn languages, English in many cases.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.580
And so they sort of, they want to expose
their kids to as much English as possible

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:33.960
when they're three years old or something,
they really pushed this early immersion.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:37.800
And my experience has been that if
the kid is interested, that's fine.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.080
But if the kid isn't interest, don't
push because it's never too late.

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.770
If somebody gets motivated at age 16,
they will, they will go and learn.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.820
And at the other end of the spectrum, you
have countries like Sweden, where all of

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.160
the television programs are in English.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:54.990
Uh, I shouldn't say they're
all in English, but they're

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:56.280
in the original version.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:59.490
So if it's an American television
program or a British television

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.630
program, all the kids are going to
watch that in the original version.

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:06.750
So Swedish kids, without even trying,
they show up at school, they already have

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.880
a fair level in English just through all
of that input, but you would not have

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:16.140
had that because in Russia you would not
have heard so much English on television.

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:17.700
Definitely not.

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:18.810
No.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:24.210
So you would have to make a special effort
to get that input, uh, through whatever

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.950
you can find on the internet and so forth.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.190
Yeah.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.960
So the podcasts that you
listen to is that mostly for

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.150
pronunciation, is it for vocabulary?

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:37.220
Uh, how much opportunity did
you have to use the language?

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:47.610
Um, I think right now and when I was like
16 or 18, I primarily focused more...

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:47.880
okay.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:53.880
When I was like 16, 18, I focus more on
the podcasts that actually taught English.

00:06:54.364 --> 00:06:58.174
They were, yeah, the hosts
were native speakers of, for

00:06:58.174 --> 00:06:59.555
example, like American English.

00:06:59.854 --> 00:07:02.854
And they would explain some
expressions in English.

00:07:03.185 --> 00:07:06.515
They would talk about the culture
and it was really interesting

00:07:06.515 --> 00:07:12.005
to me, but right now I never
listened to these podcasts anymore.

00:07:12.005 --> 00:07:15.455
I just listened to podcasts
for native speakers news.

00:07:15.544 --> 00:07:19.715
I love listening to the news
sometimes and just some interesting

00:07:19.715 --> 00:07:23.104
podcasts about anything, really,
anything I'm interested in.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:27.590
Is it easier to find, I presume it's
easier to find the content of interest

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:32.770
in English than in Chinese, for example,
or maybe there's lots of Chinese.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.630
I'm not learning Chinese right now I
don't know, maybe there's lots of stuff.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:42.590
It's easier, I would say it's easier
and faster to, to, uh, find some new

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.480
content in English than in Chinese.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.700
But if you know where to look, then it's
no problem finding anything in Chinese.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:55.724
So if a person wants to achieve a high
level, without having the opportunity to

00:07:55.724 --> 00:08:00.075
travel to where the language is spoken,
even though that might be a long-term goal

00:08:00.075 --> 00:08:04.185
to go to the country where the language
is spoken, that there is a lot that

00:08:04.185 --> 00:08:08.715
they can do on their own, and that it's
possible to achieve a very high level.

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:12.825
And what do you think are the,
the main ingredients in, in order

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:17.725
to be successful in order to
achieve a high level in language?

00:08:18.344 --> 00:08:22.515
Uh, without having the opportunity to
be in the country, what, what, what

00:08:22.515 --> 00:08:26.655
would you say if you had to name, say
three things that are essential in order

00:08:26.655 --> 00:08:27.974
to be successful, what would they be?

00:08:28.515 --> 00:08:31.875
Um, being consistent is definitely
going to be one of them.

00:08:31.965 --> 00:08:36.164
And I think it applies to everything we
do in life, not just learning a language.

00:08:36.194 --> 00:08:36.495
Yeah.

00:08:36.525 --> 00:08:39.944
If you want to be good at sports,
you need to be consistent too.

00:08:40.635 --> 00:08:47.070
Uh, so yeah, it would be consistency,
focusing on the input and I think the

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:51.900
third one is also going to be about
something about not stressing too much,

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:56.850
not worrying too much because the thoughts
we have in our head sometimes for example,

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:01.920
uh, it didn't help me at all when I was
younger to think that my English was not

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.270
good, that I couldn't communicate with
people because I would always feel scared

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.310
and not ready to talk to other people.

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.610
So yeah, definitely the thoughts
we have, our mental state

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:13.080
is really, really important.

00:09:14.745 --> 00:09:16.455
Well, you know, I
completely agree with you.

00:09:16.635 --> 00:09:21.255
Uh, we've got to give ourselves
credit for what we achieve and not

00:09:21.945 --> 00:09:25.725
criticize ourselves because we can
always improve, but we all, we will

00:09:25.725 --> 00:09:30.315
also continue making mistakes and
having things we don't understand.

00:09:30.315 --> 00:09:33.735
And none of that matters if we want it
to be a hundred percent comfortable,

00:09:33.795 --> 00:09:35.205
we stay in our native language.

00:09:35.535 --> 00:09:35.775
Yeah.

00:09:35.865 --> 00:09:38.475
Anytime we're speaking in
another language, it's, it's less

00:09:38.475 --> 00:09:40.085
comfortable, but it's more fun.

00:09:40.385 --> 00:09:40.695
Yeah.

00:09:40.695 --> 00:09:45.915
And I think you're a proof that
great things can be achieved, even if

00:09:45.915 --> 00:09:47.445
you're not surrounded by the language.

00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:48.525
So thank you.

00:09:48.525 --> 00:09:51.645
I hope that your example
can, uh, encourage people.

00:09:52.155 --> 00:09:57.045
And, uh, if we have, uh, as I do have
viewers who are not native speakers

00:09:57.045 --> 00:10:00.765
of English, uh, they may want to
check out your YouTube channel,

00:10:00.855 --> 00:10:02.775
uh, English with Veronika Mark.

00:10:03.195 --> 00:10:07.845
And I'm sure they'll find many useful
tips as well as lots of encouragement.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:12.839
So, thank you very much and good luck
in your language learning journeys.

00:10:13.199 --> 00:10:14.160
Thank you so much.

00:10:14.219 --> 00:10:14.819
Thank you.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:15.750
Bye-bye.

00:10:15.959 --> 00:10:16.199
Bye.

